[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Jack Dorsey and his company Block just released a very exciting new project called Buzz. It\'s a full-blown open-source workplace platform very similar to Slack, which aims to change the way humans and AI agents work together in one common workspace. I'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tested it out myself and it\'s honestly really interesting. So, in today\'s video, we\'ll take a look at Buzz, see how it works, and we\'ll test it out by making a joint human plus AI agent workspace and run some fun experiments'

[00:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on it. It\'s going to be a lot of fun, so let\'s dive into it. Okay, so what is Buzz? On the surface, it looks very much like Slack. You\'ve'

[00:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'got channels, threads, DMs, media sharing, search. The interface will feel instantly familiar to anyone who\'s used a modern team chat tool before. But, the interesting part is that Buzz is built'

[00:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on Nostr, the same decentralized protocol Jack Dorsey has been backing for years. His own Bluetooth mesh app BitChat eventually adopted Nostr, too, as the fallback transport for where'

[01:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there\'s no Bluetooth in range. I actually did a video on BitChat 1 year ago, and fun fact, it was my very first video after joining Better Stack. So, go check that out if you\'re interested.'

[01:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But, anyway, the way Nostr protocol works is that everything is recorded as a signed event. Messages, reactions, code changes, approvals, all of it lands in one shared searchable audit log. And'

[01:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here\'s where the agent thing on Buzz gets very clever. Every human and every agent on the platform gets its own cryptographic key pair independent of the platform itself. But, the agent\'s'

[01:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actions also carry a second signature tying it back to its human owner. So, you end up with what Block describes as a cryptographic paper trail that neither the human nor the agent could produce on'

[01:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'their own. Basically, a verifiable passport for your AI. You can always prove which agent did what and who it was working for. And there are three things that make Buzz different from any other similar platforms. First, it\'s'

[02:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'model and agent agnostic. It works with Claude code, OpenAI\'s Codex, and Block\'s own Goose framework. It communicates through the agent client protocol, which is an open standard for wiring coding'

[02:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'agents into dev tools. So, basically, you\'re not locked into one vendor\'s assistant. And second, it has Git hosting built directly into it. There\'s a software forge baked into the app, and every feature branch can become its own'

[02:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'channel. So, all your patches, your CI results, and your review comments all live in the same place as the conversation that produced them. And that\'s the part that\'s aimed squarely at GitHub, and Dorsey described Buzz as a'

[02:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tool to reduce their dependency on Slack and GitHub. And third, you own the infrastructure. It\'s Apache 2.0 licensed, the source is on GitHub, and you can either run it on your own Nostr'

[03:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'relay or use Block\'s hosted version, which is currently in beta and is absolutely free to use. So, basically, you own your data, your relay, and your agents. And currently, it\'s on version 0.4.something,'

[03:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'so it\'s still very early in its development. So, let\'s go ahead and spin it up and see how it actually works. So, the first thing I did was open buzz.x.y.z, just download the app or go to the'

[03:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'source and compile it from scratch. Either version worked perfectly fine for me. So, the first thing you\'re presented when you open the app is a button to create your own identity key. And that\'s the key difference when you\'re using the'

[03:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Nostr protocol. There are no accounts, it\'s just one key, and that is your identity, which belongs to you and not Buzz. There\'s no password to reset, and Buzz can\'t recover your key if you lose it, so keep it in a safe space. Then,'

[03:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'once you\'ve created your identity key, you\'ll be presented with an option to connect your agent harness, and then they are added to your path. And then you can also choose your default harness and model combo. And on the next step,'

[04:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'ll be asked to sign in an existing community or create your own. If you create your own, you\'ll need to log in a relay server. You could technically host your own relay server, but for'

[04:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'simplicity\'s sake of today\'s demo, I\'m going to be using Blocks own hosted relay, which does require signing up for. So, I went ahead and created an account there, but that is an account with the relay provider, not with Buzz.'

[04:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, that\'s the distinction. So, your identity key is still yours. And once that\'s done, we can finally create our own community. And for this demo, I\'m going to name mine something like Code'

[04:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Dojo. And next, we need to create our profile. For this demo, I\'ll be using the persona of the famous Silicon Valley character Richard Hendricks, just for fun. And next, you get this window where we see that we\'ll be starting out with'

[05:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'three AI agents to begin with, Fizz, Honey, and Bumble. And now, once we launch the community, you can see that we are immediately greeted with these three helper agents in our welcome channel. So, right off the bat, you already have a small team of AI agents'

[05:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in your local community ready to help you out with different types of tasks. And as we can see here, each one of them specializes in different fields. And if we want, we can also create our own custom agents. So, in my case, I\'ll'

[05:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'create our first agent, which I\'ll name Bertram Gilfoyle, keeping with the theme of Silicon Valley. And for the instructions, we\'re just going to say that you\'re a skilled web developer focused on writing clean code and paying'

[05:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'attention to security practices. And for For harness, I will actually use Blocks on Goose because this allows me to hook up external model providers. And in this case, I\'ll be using OpenRouter, and I\'ll'

[05:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'give Gilfoyle the new Gemini 3.6 model. And that\'s it for our first agent. And once you create your agent, you also get their identity key, so you should always save it in case you need to retrieve it'

[06:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'later. So, the demo today is going to be a competition between two models to see who can build the best authentication system. So, I\'m going to go ahead and create another agent, and we\'ll give this one the personality of Dinesh from'

[06:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Silicon Valley. The instructions will be fairly similar, and for the model, I\'ll give Dinesh GLM 5.2. Next, I\'m going to create a new channel called Coding Challenge. And here, I\'m'

[06:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to put these two agents to work in a showdown to create the best auth system application. So, they each need to create a separate app without using any third-party providers and provide a working demo at the end of the task. And'

[06:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'when you see these eyes and comment emojis, that means that the agents have acknowledged your request and started working on it. And you can also see this here down below. And if you click on View Activity, you can get a detailed'

[06:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'log of what the agent is currently doing. Now, for some reason, Gilfoyle, or Gemini Flash in this case, returned an empty response, so I had to ask Gilfoyle if he understood the task.'

[07:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And this is genuinely hilarious. Look at what kind of response Gilfoyle sent me. Understood, Richard. Building a proper custom auth system with blah blah blah. Unlike whatever fragile hack Dinesh is cooking up, mine will actually be'

[07:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'secure. I love how Gemini actually applied the spicy Gilfoyle persona for this agent. I love it so much that I\'m actually going to react with a joy emoji here. Anyway, while our devs are busy cooking the'

[07:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'apps, I\'m going to go ahead and create a third agent. This one is going to be Hackerman, and Hackerman is a highly skilled white hat hacker who specializes in cybersecurity, and their expertise is'

[07:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'running security audits. And I\'ll give Hackerman the Codex harness with the default model, which in this case is GPT-5.6 Soul. So, basically, I want Hackerman to analyze both codebases'

[07:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cooked up by Dinesh and Gilfoyle, and determine which one is the most secure one, which in turn will let us know which model is the superior one in this case, Gemini 3.6 Flash or GLM 5.2. And while I was setting up Hackerman, I see'

[08:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that Gilfoyle has already finished the task, so that was quick, and delivered a nice working web app with a cool stylish design. So, that\'s pretty cool. And another interesting thing to note here'

[08:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is that Buzz puts all your projects in the .buzz directory, so that\'s where all the work done by the agents live. I\'m not going to test the whole flow myself. I will let Hackerman be the judge of that, but before that, I need to get'

[08:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Dinesh\'s app ready. So, Dinesh\'s first result gave me a startup script that failed on my machine, plus it had two security vulnerabilities. Ooh. So, that\'s not a good start for GLM 5.2,'

[08:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but nonetheless, I prompted Dinesh to fix these errors, and on the second iteration, we got back a working result. Albeit, this one isn\'t too exciting design-wise, but it looks like it\'s functional, so that\'s good. So, now I\'m'

[09:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to ask Hackerman to review both of the apps and determine which one is superior in terms of security practices. And a few minutes later, we finally get the result. And to my surprise, Dinesh\'s app was actually ranked higher than'

[09:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Gilfoyle\'s. Narrowly, but still. So, judging by the result, GLM 5.2 beats Gemini 3.6 Flash. Well, at least in terms of security practices, but we also'

[09:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'got to take into account the two iterations GLM had to do, plus the vulnerable NPM packages it included. But on the other hand, if we look at Open Router\'s cost metrics, Gemini Flash is just really, really expensive compared'

[09:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to GLM. So, combining all of that, I would say GLM 5.2 comes out being the winner in the end. But now I had decided to do something really funny. I asked Dinesh and Gilfoyle what are their'

[09:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thoughts about the results, and as soon as I did that, the agents started arguing like crazy. Mainly Gilfoyle and Hackerman had spicier takes and more heated arguments about the results.'

[10:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Dinesh was kind of diplomatic in its answers, but it was honestly so freaking hilarious to see how these agents got in a debate about the results of the test. And another cool thing is that these agents retain a memory of their chats in'

[10:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the community, so they get better over time within the Buzz ecosystem. For example, you might encounter situations where if you\'re using one of these Open Router models for your agents, they might end up searching the inner Buzz'

[10:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'docs to see how to execute certain commands, like adding others to channels or similar actions. But once they\'ve gone through it, next time they are able to do it in one shot. So, overall, I'

[10:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'would say that this is a very interesting concept, having a Slack-like workspace for you and your agents. But while testing it, I also came across situations where I exceeded my token limits because the problem with these'

[10:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'multi-agent workflows is that they keep messaging each other and piling requests upon each other until you max out your tokens. So, what I learned is that you got to set strict limits for your agents when to do a hard stop on a task,'

[11:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'otherwise they can just go off the rails. So, there you have it, folks. That is Buzz in a nutshell. It\'s a cool app, and it has a really nice design, and I had so much fun playing around with these agents in a Slack look-alike'

[11:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'interface. But I would say that the app itself is still very early in its infancy. I guess the big pitch here is that this software might become the new place for both Slack-type communication'

[11:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and a single hub for managing Git branches, YAML workflows, and even Git hosting. But, will people actually latch onto it? I think it\'s too early to tell. But, what do you think about Buzz? Have'

[11:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you tried it? Will you use it? Let us know in the comments section down below. And folks, if you like these types of technical breakdowns, please let me know by smashing that like button underneath the video. And also, don\'t forget to'

